use std
into report.amni

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with rp_ is exported). It is Amni-CAD's report writer: from one report description (JSON text) plus the JPEG and PNG images and the result numbers that description points at, it writes the report as a PDF, a Word document (.docx), a self-contained HTML page, a CSV summary or a CSV of result data. Layout lengths are PDF points (72 per inch); colors are three bytes red, green, blue.

```amni
import json;
import base64;
extern fn rp_view_u8(id i32, xs u8[:]) = "rp_view_u8";
extern fn rp_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "rp_view_f64";
let HELV int[224] = [278, 278, 355, 556, 556, 889, 667, 191, 333, 333, 389, 584, 278, 333, 278, 278, 556, 556, 556, 556, 556, 556, 556, 556, 556, 556, 278, 278, 584, 584, 584, 556, 1015, 667, 667, 722, 722, 667, 611, 778, 722, 278, 500, 667, 556, 833, 722, 778, 667, 778, 722, 667, 611, 722, 667, 944, 667, 667, 611, 278, 278, 278, 469, 556, 333, 556, 556, 500, 556, 556, 278, 556, 556, 222, 222, 500, 222, 833, 556, 556, 556, 556, 333, 500, 278, 556, 500, 722, 500, 500, 500, 334, 260, 334, 584, 350, 556, 350, 222, 556, 333, 1000, 556, 556, 333, 1000, 667, 333, 1000, 350, 611, 350, 350, 222, 222, 333, 333, 350, 556, 1000, 333, 1000, 500, 333, 944, 350, 500, 667, 278, 333, 556, 556, 556, 556, 260, 556, 333, 737, 370, 556, 584, 333, 737, 333, 400, 584, 333, 333, 333, 556, 537, 278, 333, 333, 365, 556, 834, 834, 834, 611, 667, 667, 667, 667, 667, 667, 1000, 722, 667, 667, 667, 667, 278, 278, 278, 278, 722, 722, 778, 778, 778, 778, 778, 584, 778, 722, 722, 722, 722, 667, 667, 611, 556, 556, 556, 556, 556, 556, 889, 500, 556, 556, 556, 556, 278, 278, 278, 278, 556, 556, 556, 556, 556, 556, 556, 584, 611, 556, 556, 556, 556, 500, 556, 500];
let HELVB int[224] = [278, 333, 474, 556, 556, 889, 722, 238, 333, 333, 389, 584, 278, 333, 278, 278, 556, 556, 556, 556, 556, 556, 556, 556, 556, 556, 333, 333, 584, 584, 584, 611, 975, 722, 722, 722, 722, 667, 611, 778, 722, 278, 556, 722, 611, 833, 722, 778, 667, 778, 722, 667, 611, 722, 667, 944, 667, 667, 611, 333, 278, 333, 584, 556, 333, 556, 611, 556, 611, 556, 333, 611, 611, 278, 278, 556, 278, 889, 611, 611, 611, 611, 389, 556, 333, 611, 556, 778, 556, 556, 500, 389, 280, 389, 584, 350, 556, 350, 278, 556, 500, 1000, 556, 556, 333, 1000, 667, 333, 1000, 350, 611, 350, 350, 278, 278, 500, 500, 350, 556, 1000, 333, 1000, 556, 333, 944, 350, 500, 667, 278, 333, 556, 556, 556, 556, 280, 556, 333, 737, 370, 556, 584, 333, 737, 333, 400, 584, 333, 333, 333, 611, 556, 278, 333, 333, 365, 556, 834, 834, 834, 611, 722, 722, 722, 722, 722, 722, 1000, 722, 667, 667, 667, 667, 278, 278, 278, 278, 722, 722, 778, 778, 778, 778, 778, 584, 778, 722, 722, 722, 722, 667, 667, 611, 556, 556, 556, 556, 556, 556, 889, 556, 556, 556, 556, 556, 278, 278, 278, 278, 611, 611, 611, 611, 611, 611, 611, 584, 611, 611, 611, 611, 611, 556, 611, 556];
let WINHI int[32] = [8364, 0, 8218, 402, 8222, 8230, 8224, 8225, 710, 8240, 352, 8249, 338, 0, 381, 0, 0, 8216, 8217, 8220, 8221, 8226, 8211, 8212, 732, 8482, 353, 8250, 339, 0, 382, 376];
let SYM int[246] = [913, 65, 722, 914, 66, 667, 915, 71, 603, 916, 68, 612, 917, 69, 611, 918, 90, 611, 919, 72, 722, 920, 81, 741, 921, 73, 333, 922, 75, 722, 923, 76, 686, 924, 77, 889, 925, 78, 722, 926, 88, 645, 927, 79, 722, 928, 80, 768, 929, 82, 556, 931, 83, 592, 932, 84, 611, 933, 85, 690, 934, 70, 763, 935, 67, 722, 936, 89, 795, 937, 87, 768, 945, 97, 631, 946, 98, 549, 947, 103, 411, 948, 100, 494, 949, 101, 439, 950, 122, 494, 951, 104, 603, 952, 113, 521, 953, 105, 329, 954, 107, 549, 955, 108, 549, 956, 109, 576, 957, 110, 521, 958, 120, 493, 959, 111, 549, 960, 112, 549, 961, 114, 549, 962, 86, 439, 963, 115, 603, 964, 116, 439, 965, 117, 576, 966, 102, 521, 967, 99, 549, 968, 121, 686, 969, 119, 686, 977, 74, 631, 981, 106, 603, 8486, 87, 768, 8710, 68, 612, 8722, 45, 549, 8869, 94, 658, 8764, 126, 549, 8242, 162, 247, 8243, 178, 411, 8804, 163, 549, 8734, 165, 713, 8596, 171, 1042, 8592, 172, 987, 8593, 173, 603, 8594, 174, 987, 8595, 175, 603, 8805, 179, 549, 8733, 181, 713, 8706, 182, 494, 8800, 185, 549, 8801, 186, 549, 8776, 187, 549, 8709, 198, 823, 8712, 206, 713, 8736, 208, 768, 8711, 209, 713, 8719, 213, 823, 8730, 214, 549, 8901, 215, 250, 8743, 217, 603, 8744, 218, 603, 8721, 229, 713, 8747, 242, 274];
```

The report description is a JSON object; every member is optional. "title", "subtitle", "header" (short text at the top right of every page after the first), "footer" (text at the bottom left of every page), "paper" ("a4" for ISO A4, anything else for US Letter), "stamp" (the UTC creation time as 14 digits YYYYMMDDhhmmss), "meta" (an array of [label, value] string pairs shown under the title) and "blocks" (an array of block objects drawn in order). Every block has a kind in "t": "h" (numbered section heading, "text"), "h2" (subheading, "text"), "p" (paragraph, "text"), "note" (small grey italic paragraph, "text"), "verdict" ("level" is "ok", "warn" or "err", plus "text"), "kv" ("rows": [label, value] pairs), "table" ("cols": header strings, "rows": arrays of strings, optional "cap"), "fig" ("imgs": image references, each with "cap"), "scale" (a color bar: "label", "stops" as [r, g, b] arrays of 0 to 255, "lo" and "hi" end labels, optional "mid" array of labels spaced evenly between them), "chart" (an x-y line chart: "xl" and "yl" axis labels, optional "cap", "series" array of objects with "name", "x" and "y" number arrays and optional boolean "dots"; a chart or scale may also carry "png", an image reference of the same picture rendered by the page, used only for the Word document) and "br" (page break). An image reference is an object with "off" and "len" (its bytes inside the image buffer), "w" and "h" (its size in pixels) and "fmt" ("png" for PNG, anything else JPEG). "data" is an object with "cols" (column names) and "rows" (a row count): the data buffer holds that many rows of one real per column, row after row. Text is UTF-8.

Module state: `IN` (a growable byte list) (the JSON text), `IMG` (a growable byte list) (the image bytes), `DAT` (a growable real list) (the data numbers), `OUT` (a growable byte list) (the result), `D json::Doc` and `ROOT int` (the parsed description and its root node, -1 when none), `BLK int` (the "blocks" array node or -1). `refresh_views()` publishes the four buffers with `rp_view_u8(1, IN)`, `rp_view_u8(2, IMG)`, `rp_view_f64(3, DAT)` and `rp_view_u8(4, OUT)`; call it at the end of every exported function, because any growth moves the buffers. Exports: `rp_in(n i32)`, `rp_img(n i32)` and `rp_dat(n i32)` each empty their buffer, give it `reserve`(n) and resize it to n zeros, then refresh the views. `rp_make(fmt i32) i32` writes the report in format fmt (1 PDF, 2 Word document, 3 HTML, 4 CSV summary, 5 CSV data) into OUT and returns its length; `rp_svg(k i32) i32` writes the standalone SVG picture of block k (counting from 0) into OUT and returns its length, or -1 when block k is not a "chart" or "scale". Both first call `prepare()`, which parses IN; when parsing fails they put the text "report: " followed by the parser's message into OUT and return -1.

`prepare() bool` calls `reset()`, then `json::parse(IN[..], D)`, keeping the root in ROOT; on failure it writes the message into OUT as described and returns false. Then it sets BLK to the "blocks" member of the root (-1 when missing or not an array) and calls `set_paper()`. `reset()` empties every work array of this file and gives each one `reserve` right after emptying it: OUT gets `reserve`(IN.len plus IMG.len times 2 plus DAT.len times 24 plus 65536), PC and PART get `reserve`(1048576), DN gets `reserve`(65536), DTX, OT, ZN and LS get `reserve`(16384), DL gets `reserve`(4096), PS, MK, IM, ZE, XO, TW, TQ and TM get `reserve`(256); it sets every counter of this file back to 0.

JSON helpers (all read the module's D; nodes are indices into `D.nodes`): `jm(n int, key) int` is `json::member(D, n, key)`; `js(n int, key) bytes` is the text of member key when it is a string (`json::text_of`), else the empty string; `jn(n int, key, dflt real) real` is the member's number (`D.nodes[m].num`) when it is a number (kind `json::K_INT` or `json::K_NUM`), else dflt; `jb(n int, key) bool` is true when the member is a boolean whose `whole` is 1; `jcount(n int) int` is `D.nodes[n].count` when n is an array (`json::K_ARR`), else 0. Arrays are always walked by following `D.nodes[n].first` and then each child's `next` until -1, never by index. `jtext(n int) bytes` is the text of node n when it is a string; when it is a number it is the shortest form of the number (std `format`) written into the module scratch `TB` (a growable byte list) (emptied first) and returned as a slice of TB; anything else gives the empty string.

Text encoding. `utf8_at(s bytes, i int) int, int` decodes the UTF-8 code point starting at byte i and returns it with the index after it; a malformed or cut-off sequence gives the code point 63 and the next index i plus 1. `glyph(cp int, font int) int, int, int` maps a code point to the PDF font, the byte and the width in thousandths of the font size. Fonts are 0 Helvetica, 1 Helvetica-Bold, 2 Helvetica-Oblique and 3 Symbol; the width table for fonts 0 and 2 is `HELV` and for font 1 `HELVB`, both indexed by byte minus 32. Tab and the code points 8194, 8195, 8201 and 8239 become the space byte 32; 8208 and 8209 become the hyphen byte 45; 8960 (the diameter sign) becomes byte 216. Shapes the fonts lack fall back to near look-alikes: 9679 and 9632 (black circle and square) become byte 149 (the bullet); 9675 and 9633 (white circle and square) become byte 111 ("o"); 10005, 10006, 10007 and 10008 (X marks) become byte 215 (the multiplication sign); 9733 and 9734 (stars) become byte 42 (the asterisk). 10003 and 10004 (check marks) are font 3 with byte 214 and width 549 (the radical sign). Code points 32 to 126 and 160 to 255 are their own byte. A code point equal to `WINHI`[k] (k from 0 to 31, skipping entries that are 0) is byte 128 plus k. Otherwise when the code point appears at `SYM`[3j] (the list holds triplets: code point, Symbol byte, width) the result is font 3 with byte `SYM`[3j+1] and width `SYM`[3j+2]. Anything else is the question mark byte 63 in the given font. For fonts 0 to 2 the width is the table entry of the byte.

`text_w(s bytes, font int, size real) real` is the sum of the glyph widths of s divided by 1000 and multiplied by size; line feeds count 0.

`pnum(out (a byte list it appends to), v real)` writes v for PDF and SVG: std `format_flt` with 2 decimals, then trailing zeros after the point and a trailing point removed; "-0" becomes "0". `pcol(out (a byte list it appends to), c int, stroke bool)` writes the three components of the color c (0xRRGGBB) as fractions of 255 with 3 decimals each (same trimming), separated by spaces, then " RG\n" when stroke else " rg\n". `hex6(out (a byte list it appends to), c int)` writes c as six uppercase hexadecimal digits.

`pdf_str(out (a byte list it appends to), s bytes, font int)` writes the glyph bytes of s in the given font as PDF text operators: it walks the code points, groups consecutive glyphs that map to the same PDF font, and for each group writes "/F" followed by the font number plus 1, a space, the current size `FS` (module real, set by the caller) via `pnum`, " Tf (", the bytes with a backslash before every byte 40, 41 or 92, then ") Tj\n". Line feeds are skipped. `pdf_text(x real, y real, s bytes, font int, size real, c int, anchor int)` writes "BT\n", the fill color, then "x y Td\n" where x is moved left by the text width times 0.5 when anchor is 1 (center) or by the whole width when anchor is 2 (right), sets `FS` to size, calls `pdf_str` and writes "ET\n". All page drawing goes to the page content buffer `PC` (a growable byte list).

Page geometry (module reals): `PW` and `PH` (page size), `X0`, `X1` (left and right content edges), `TOP` and `BOT` (highest and lowest content baseline room), `CW` (content width X1 minus X0) and `Y` (the layout cursor, a PDF y that moves down the page). `set_paper()` reads "paper" from the root: "a4" gives 595.28 by 841.89, anything else 612 by 792; then X0 is 54, X1 is PW minus 54, TOP is PH minus 66, BOT is 58 and CW is X1 minus X0. Colors used throughout: ink 0x1C1F24, grey 0x5B6370, rule 0xD5D9DF, dark rule 0x8A919C, accent 0xC89B4E, header fill 0xEEF0F3, zebra fill 0xF7F8FA.

Pages are laid out one after another into PC; `PS` (a growable int list) holds the PC offset where each page starts and `NP int` counts pages. `new_page()` pushes PC.len onto PS, adds 1 to NP and sets Y to TOP. On every page after the first it also writes the running header: the root's "title" at (X0, PH minus 40) in font 0 size 8 grey, the root's "header" right-aligned at (X1, PH minus 40) in font 0 size 8 grey, and a 0.5 wide rule line from (X0, PH minus 46) to (X1, PH minus 46). `need(h real)` calls `new_page()` when Y minus h is below BOT. A line is drawn with `pdf_line(x1 real, y1 real, x2 real, y2 real, w real, c int)`, which writes the stroke color, then the width followed by " w ", then "x1 y1 m x2 y2 l S\n" (numbers via `pnum`). `pdf_rect(x real, y real, w real, h real, c int)` writes the fill color then "x y w h re f\n".

Wrapping. `wrap(s bytes, font int, size real, width real) int` fills the module list `LS` (a growable int list) (emptied first) with pairs of byte offsets, the start and end of each line, and returns the line count. Text is split at line feeds; each piece is filled greedily: words are separated by spaces, a line takes as many words as fit within width (measured with `text_w`, the spaces between words included), a word wider than width on its own is cut at the last byte that still fits (always at least one code point per line, never inside a UTF-8 sequence), and spaces at the start or end of a line are not part of it. An empty piece still gives one empty line. `para(s bytes, font int, size real, lead real, c int, x real, width real, anchor int)` wraps s at width and, for each line, calls `need(lead)`, moves Y down by lead and draws the line with `pdf_text` at Y (at x for anchor 0, x plus width over 2 for anchor 1, x plus width for anchor 2).

Counters (module ints): `NSEC` (sections so far), `NFIG` (figures), `NTAB` (tables). Outline marks: `struct Mark { at int, len int, page int, y real }` in `MK` (a growable list of Mark), whose text lives in `OT` (a growable byte list) (at and len index OT). `pdf_title_block()` starts the first page with `new_page()`, then draws the root's "title" with `para` in font 1 size 20 lead 25 ink, the "subtitle" (when not empty) in font 0 size 11 lead 15 grey, moves Y down 8, draws "meta" as a key-value table with `kv_table` when it has rows, then draws a 2 wide accent line from (X0, Y minus 6) to (X1, Y minus 6) and moves Y down 18.

`pdf_heading(b int, level int)`: for level 1 it adds 1 to NSEC and the text is NSEC, ". " and the block's "text"; level 2 uses the text alone. `keep_h(b int) real` is the room the blocks right after block b need to stay on its page: starting at `D.nodes[b].next` and following `.next` (stop at -1), add 24 for an "h2" and 50 for a "scale" and keep walking; for a "fig" add `fig_h` of it and stop; for a "chart" add 234 and stop; any other kind stops the walk without adding. The heading calls `need(` the smaller of TOP minus BOT and the larger of 64 and (36 for level 1, 24 for level 2) plus `keep_h(b)` `)` so a heading is never left alone at the bottom of a page and stays with the color scales and the first picture that follow it, then moves Y down 10 for level 1 (6 for level 2), then for level 1 pushes a Mark (the heading text appended to OT, the page index NP minus 1, y equal to Y plus 4) and draws the text with `para` in font 1, size 13 and lead 17 for level 1 or size 11 and lead 15 for level 2, ink color, at X0 across CW. Level 1 then draws a 0.75 wide accent line from (X0, Y minus 5) to (X1, Y minus 5) and moves Y down 9; level 2 moves Y down 3.

`pdf_par(b int)` draws the block's "text" with `para` in font 0 size 10 lead 13.5 ink and then moves Y down 6. `pdf_note(b int)` uses font 2 size 8.5 lead 11 grey and then moves Y down 5. `pdf_verdict(b int)`: the bar and fill colors are 0x2E9E5B and 0xE5F5EC for "ok", 0xC98A0B and 0xFDF3DC for "warn", 0xD33A3A and 0xFDE7E7 for "err"; it wraps the text in font 1 size 10 at CW minus 22, the box height is the line count times 13.5 plus 12; `need` that height plus 8, move Y down 4, fill the box (X0, Y minus height, CW, height) with the fill color and a 3 wide bar (X0, Y minus height, 3, height) with the bar color, then draw each line in ink at X0 plus 12 with a baseline 13.5 lower than the last (the first at Y minus 6 minus 10.5), then move Y down by the height plus 8.

Tables. `cell(row int, c int) bytes` is `jtext` of element c of the row array (walking the links), or empty. `ncols(cols int, rows int) int` is the element count of cols when cols is an array with elements, else the largest element count among the rows. `col_widths(cols int, rows int, kv bool, width real)` fills `TW` (a growable real list) (column widths), `TQ` (a growable real list) (minimum widths) and `TM` (a growable int list) (1 for a numeric column, else 0), all emptied first, with one entry per column, all at size 9. A text's natural width is the widest of its line-feed-separated pieces (`text_w`) plus 10 and its word width is its widest space-separated word plus 10. TW starts as the largest natural width in the column (header cells in font 1; body cells in font 0, except the first column of a key-value table in font 1) and TQ as the largest word width, at most 110. A table column is numeric when it has at least one non-empty body cell and every non-empty body cell begins, after an optional plus, minus or U+2212 sign, with a digit or with a point followed by a digit; key-value tables never are. Key-value tables then get column 0 the smaller of its natural width and 0.42 times width, and column 1 the rest. Other tables: with total the sum of TW, when total is at most width every column grows by (width minus total) times its share of total; otherwise each column becomes the larger of TW times width over total and the smaller of its TQ and TW, and then all columns are scaled so they add up to width.

`row_h(row int, kv bool, bold bool) real` is the largest line count of the row's cells (wrapped at the column width minus 10 in font 1 when bold or when it is the first column of a key-value table, else font 0, size 9) times 11.5, plus 8. `pdf_row(row int, kv bool, bold bool, fill int, rule real, rc int)` draws one row whose top is Y: when fill is not -1 it fills (X0, Y minus the row height, CW, row height) with fill; each cell is wrapped as in `row_h` and its lines drawn in ink with the first baseline at Y minus 12.5 and each next one 11.5 lower, right-aligned at the cell's right edge minus 5 for a numeric column, else starting at its left edge plus 5; then it draws a line of width rule and color rc under the row from X0 to X1 and moves Y down by the row height. `pdf_grid(cols int, rows int, kv bool, cap bytes)`: when cap is not empty it adds 1 to NTAB, calls `need(` 40 `)`, moves Y down 4 and draws "Table ", NTAB, ". " and cap with `para` in font 1 size 9 lead 12 ink. Then `col_widths` at CW; when cols has elements and kv is false it needs the header row height plus the first row's height and draws the header row (bold, header fill, rule 0.75 dark rule). Each body row k (counting from 0): when it does not fit above BOT it calls `new_page()` and draws the header row again (when there is one); it is drawn with the zebra fill when k is odd (else no fill), not bold, rule 0.4 in the rule color. Finally Y moves down 8. `kv_table(rows int)` is `pdf_grid(-1, rows, true, "")`; the "kv" block calls it with its "rows" and the "table" block calls `pdf_grid` with "cols", "rows", false and "cap".

Images. `struct Img { off int, len int, w int, h int, png bool }` in `IM` (a growable list of Img); `img_id(r int) int` returns the index in IM of the image reference node r, matching an earlier entry with the same off, else appending a new one (png true when its "fmt" is "png"). `fig_h(b int) real` measures a "fig" block without drawing anything or counting figures: with n images, gap 12, cell width cw equal to (CW minus gap times (n minus 1)) over n and maximum height (TOP minus BOT) times 0.46 for one image or 0.36 for more, each image is scaled by the smaller of cw over its pixel width and the maximum height over its pixel height; the caption of an image with a non-empty "cap" is measured as "Figure 00. " followed by cap, wrapped at cw in font 0 size 8.5; it returns the tallest scaled image height plus 6 plus the largest caption line count times 11 plus 4. `pdf_fig(b int)` lays out the block's "imgs" side by side: with n images, gap 12, cell width cw equal to (CW minus gap times (n minus 1)) over n and maximum height (TOP minus BOT) times 0.46 for one image or 0.36 for more, each image is scaled by the smaller of cw over its pixel width and the maximum height over its pixel height. Every image with a non-empty "cap" gets the next figure number (add 1 to NFIG) and the caption "Figure ", the number, ". " and cap, wrapped at cw in font 0 size 8.5; the caption height is the largest caption line count times 11. Call `need(` the tallest image plus 6 plus the caption height plus 4 `)` and move Y down 2. An image whose "fmt" is "png" is not drawn and gets no `img_id` (the PDF holds only JPEG pictures; its frame and caption still are). Each image i is drawn centered in its cell (left edge X0 plus i times (cw plus gap)) with its top at Y: "q dw 0 0 dh x y cm /Im" followed by its `img_id` and " Do Q\n" (dw, dh the drawn size, y equal to Y minus dh), then a 0.5 wide rule-colored frame "x y dw dh re S". Move Y down by the tallest image plus 6, draw each caption centered in its cell in grey with baselines 9, 20, 31 and so on below Y, and move Y down by the caption height plus 10.

Display list (shared by PDF and SVG): local coordinates in points with the origin at the top left and y growing downward. `struct Prim { kind int, a int, n int, t int, tl int, c int, font int, anchor int, rot int, size real }` in `DL` (a growable list of Prim), numbers in `DN` (a growable real list) (a and n index DN) and text in `DTX` (a growable byte list) (t and tl index DTX). Kinds: 1 filled rectangle (x, y, w, h), 2 line (x1, y1, x2, y2, width), 3 polyline (width then x, y pairs), 4 dot (x, y, radius), 5 text (x, y at the baseline; rot 1 means turned a quarter turn counterclockwise so it reads upward, with the text's run along the rotated baseline; anchor 0 start, 1 middle, 2 end). Helpers `dl_rect`, `dl_line`, `dl_dot` and `dl_text(x, y, s, font, size, c, anchor, rot)` append one primitive; a polyline is started with `dl_poly(width real, c int)` and points are added with `dl_pt(x, y)`, which push onto DN and add 1 to the last primitive's n.

`lerp_stops(st int, t real) int` interpolates the color array st (its elements are [r, g, b] arrays) at t from 0 to 1: with k stops equally spaced (stop j at j over k minus 1), find the pair around t and blend each component, rounding to the nearest integer; one stop gives its color. `scale_dl(b int, width real) real` empties the display list and draws a color bar for block b: bw is the smaller of width and 360; the "label" as text at (0, 9) in font 1 size 9 ink; 96 rectangles side by side filling (0, 14) to (bw, 26), rectangle i colored `lerp_stops` at (i plus 0.5) over 96; a 0.5 wide dark-rule frame of 4 lines around the bar; tick lines 0.5 wide dark rule from y 26 to 29 at x 0, at x bw and at each "mid" position; "lo" at (0, 38) anchor 0, "hi" at (bw, 38) anchor 2 and the m "mid" labels at x equal to bw times j over (m plus 1) for j from 1, anchor 1, all font 0 size 8 grey. It returns 42 (the picture height) and stores bw in the module real `DW`.

`nice_step(span real, target int) real` is the smallest of 1, 2, 2.5 and 5 times a power of ten that is at least span over target (span above 0). `tick_text(out (a byte list it appends to), v real, step real)` writes v with std `format_flt` using as many decimals as the step needs (0 when the step is at least 1, else the count of digits after the point in the step's shortest form, at most 7), then turns "-0" into "0".

`chart_dl(b int, width real, height real)` empties the display list and draws block b's line chart in a width by height box. The data range covers every x and y of every series (series with fewer x than y use the y count up to the x count); when a range is empty (or has no data) it becomes the value minus 1 to the value plus 1 (0 to 1 without data). Each axis gets `nice_step(` range span, 5 `)` and its ends are moved out to multiples of the step (floor for the low end, ceiling for the high end). The plot area runs from L 58 to R width minus 12 across and from T (26 when any series has a non-empty "name", else 10) to B height minus 34 down. Data maps to x px equal to L plus (x minus x0) over (x1 minus x0) times (R minus L) and py equal to B minus (y minus y0) over (y1 minus y0) times (B minus T). Draw: for every y tick a 0.4 wide grid line 0xE1E4E8 from L to R and its `tick_text` at (L minus 5, py plus 3) font 0 size 8 grey anchor 2; for every x tick a grid line from T to B and its label at (px, B plus 12) anchor 1; the left and bottom axis lines 0.75 wide dark rule. Each series s gets palette color s modulo 6 from 0x2B59C3, 0xE0322E, 0x3FA34D, 0xC89B4E, 0x7B4FC9, 0x2BB5C3: a polyline 1.5 wide through its points unless "dots" is true, and a dot of radius 2 at each point when "dots" is true or it has a single point. When any series has a non-empty "name", the legend is one row above the plot ending at R, laid out from right to left in reverse series order: each named series takes its name (font 0 size 8 grey, anchor 2) ending at the current right edge with its baseline at 13, then 4 points to the left of the name a 14 long sample ending there: a 2 wide line in its color at height 10, or a dot of radius 2.5 at its middle when "dots" is true; the right edge then moves left past the sample by 12 more. The "xl" label is centered at ((L plus R) over 2, height minus 6) and the "yl" label at (14, (T plus B) over 2) turned upward (rot 1), both font 0 size 8.5 ink anchor 1.

`dl_pdf(ox real, oy real)` writes the display list into PC with the local origin at PDF point (ox, oy), so a local point (x, y) is at (ox plus x, oy minus y). Rectangles: fill color, then "x y w h re f" with the PDF y at the rectangle's bottom. Lines: stroke color, width " w ", "m", "l", "S". Polylines: "1 J 1 j", stroke color, width, the points with "m" then "l", "S", then "0 J 0 j". Dots: a filled circle from four Bezier curves with control offset 0.5523 times the radius ("m", four "c", "f"). Text: "BT", fill color, then for rot 0 "x y Td" with the anchor shift along x; for rot 1 the matrix "0 1 -1 0 x y Tm" with the anchor shift applied to y instead; then `pdf_str` at the primitive's size and "ET".

`dl_svg(out (a byte list it appends to), w real, h real, standalone bool)` writes the display list as SVG: when standalone, first `<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>` and a line feed; then `<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="W" height="H" viewBox="0 0 W H" font-family="Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif">`, then a white rectangle covering everything, then each primitive: `<rect x y width height fill="#RRGGBB"/>`; `<line x1 y1 x2 y2 stroke="#RRGGBB" stroke-width/>`; `<polyline points="x,y x,y …" fill="none" stroke stroke-width stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-linecap="round"/>`; `<circle cx cy r fill/>`; `<text x y font-size fill>` plus ` text-anchor="middle"` or `"end"` for anchors 1 and 2, ` font-weight="bold"` for font 1, ` font-style="italic"` for font 2 and ` transform="rotate(-90 x y)"` for rot 1, then the text with `&`, `<`, `>` and `"` escaped as entities, then `</text>`; finally `</svg>`. Colors use `hex6` after a `#` (lowercase or uppercase both fine); numbers use `pnum`.

`pdf_scale(b int)`: h is `scale_dl(b, CW)`; `need(h plus 6)`, then `dl_pdf(X0, Y minus 2)` and move Y down by h plus 8. `pdf_chart(b int)`: the chart is CW wide and 220 high; when "cap" is not empty add 1 to NFIG and the caption is "Figure ", NFIG, ". " and cap, wrapped at CW in font 0 size 8.5 (11 per line); `need(` 224 plus the caption height `)`, `chart_dl(b, CW, 220)`, `dl_pdf(X0, Y minus 2)`, move Y down 224, draw the caption centered in grey across the content width with `para` and move Y down 10.

`pdf_layout()` calls `pdf_title_block()` and then walks the blocks: "h" `pdf_heading(b, 1)`, "h2" `pdf_heading(b, 2)`, "p" `pdf_par`, "note" `pdf_note`, "verdict" `pdf_verdict`, "kv" `kv_table` of its "rows", "table" as described for tables, "fig" `pdf_fig`, "scale" `pdf_scale`, "chart" `pdf_chart`, "br" `new_page()` unless Y is still TOP; unknown kinds are skipped. Finally it pushes PC.len onto PS so page p's content is PC from `PS`[p] to `PS`[p plus 1].

`pdf_lit(out (a byte list it appends to), s bytes)` writes s as a PDF text string for document info and outline titles, in UTF-16BE hex: "<FEFF", then for each code point read with `utf8_at` its UTF-16 code units as four uppercase hexadecimal digits each (a code point above 65535 becomes the pair 55296 plus ((cp minus 65536) shifted right by 10) and 56320 plus ((cp minus 65536) and 1023)), then ">".

`write_pdf()` runs `pdf_layout()` and then writes OUT. `XO` (a growable int list) collects the byte offset of each object by number (index 0 unused). Object numbers: 1 catalog, 2 page tree, 3 document info, 4 the shared resource dictionary, 5 to 8 the fonts Helvetica, Helvetica-Bold, Helvetica-Oblique and Symbol, then one per image in IM (`9 plus k`), then, when there are outline marks, the outline root followed by one object per mark, then two per page: the page and its content stream. Every object is written as the number, " 0 obj\n", the body and "\nendobj\n", recording the offset first. Write "%PDF-1.4\n%" followed by the bytes 226, 227, 207, 211 and a line feed. Catalog: "<< /Type /Catalog /Pages 2 0 R" and, with marks, " /Outlines R 0 R /PageMode /UseOutlines" (R the outline root's number), then " >>". Page tree: "<< /Type /Pages /Kids [" each page's number followed by " 0 R " "] /Count N >>". Info: "<< /Title " `pdf_lit` of the title " /Producer (Amni-CAD) /Creator (Amni-CAD)" and, when "stamp" has 14 characters, " /CreationDate (D:" stamp "Z)", then " >>". Resources: "<< /Font << /F1 5 0 R /F2 6 0 R /F3 7 0 R /F4 8 0 R >> /XObject << " then "/Im" k and its object reference for every image " >> /ProcSet [/PDF /Text /ImageC] >>". Fonts 5 to 7: "<< /Type /Font /Subtype /Type1 /BaseFont /Helvetica /Encoding /WinAnsiEncoding >>" with BaseFont Helvetica, Helvetica-Bold and Helvetica-Oblique; font 8: "<< /Type /Font /Subtype /Type1 /BaseFont /Symbol >>". Each image: "<< /Type /XObject /Subtype /Image /Width w /Height h /ColorSpace /DeviceRGB /BitsPerComponent 8 /Filter /DCTDecode /Length len >>\nstream\n", its bytes from IMG, "\nendstream". Outline root: "<< /Type /Outlines /First f 0 R /Last l 0 R /Count m >>"; mark i: "<< /Title " title " /Parent R 0 R" plus " /Prev" and " /Next" references to its neighbours when they exist, " /Dest [P 0 R /XYZ 0 y 0] >>" with P the object number of the mark's page and y via `pnum`. Page p: "<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 PW PH] /Resources 4 0 R /Contents C 0 R >>". Its content stream is the page's slice of PC followed by its footer, built in the scratch `PART` (emptied first): a 0.5 wide rule line from (X0, 46) to (X1, 46), the root's "footer" at (X0, 34) and "Page n of N" right-aligned at (X1, 34), both font 0 size 8 grey, with n the page number from 1 and N the page count; the body is "<< /Length L >>\nstream\n", the bytes, "\nendstream" with L the total byte count. Then the cross-reference table: remember its offset, write "xref\n0 " the object count plus 1, "\n0000000000 65535 f \n", then for every object its offset as ten digits with leading zeros followed by " 00000 n \n"; then "trailer\n<< /Size " count plus 1 " /Root 1 0 R /Info 3 0 R >>\nstartxref\n" the offset "\n%%EOF\n".

`xesc(out (a byte list it appends to), s bytes)` appends s with `&`, `<`, `>` and `"` written as `&amp;`, `&lt;`, `&gt;` and `&quot;`, and drops the control bytes below 32 other than tab and line feed (they are not allowed in XML).

Zip (stored, no compression). `CRC u32[256]` is the standard CRC-32 table (polynomial 0xEDB88320, reflected), filled on first use; `crc32(s bytes) u32` is the usual CRC-32 of s. `struct Zent { name int, nlen int, crc u32, size int, at int }` in `ZE` (a growable list of Zent) with the names in `ZN` (a growable byte list). `ZT int` and `ZD int` are the MS-DOS time and date of every entry: from a 14-character "stamp" the date is (year minus 1980) times 512 plus month times 32 plus day and the time is hour times 2048 plus minute times 32 plus second over 2; without a stamp the date is 33 (1980-01-01) and the time 0. `zip_add(name bytes, data bytes)` appends to OUT a local file header (little-endian: signature 0x04034B50 as 4 bytes, version 20 as 2, flags 0 as 2, method 0 as 2, time 2, date 2, crc 4, compressed size 4, size 4, name length 2, extra length 0 as 2), the name and the data, recording the entry. `zip_end()` appends the central directory, one record per entry (signature 0x02014B50, version made by 20, version needed 20, flags 0, method 0, time, date, crc, compressed size, size, name length, extra 0, comment 0, disk 0, internal attributes 0 as 2 bytes, external attributes 0 as 4, local header offset as 4, then the name), then the end record (signature 0x06054B50, disk 0, disk with directory 0, entry count, entry count, directory size as 4, directory offset as 4, comment length 0).

The fixed parts of the Word document and the HTML page, used verbatim:

```amni
let X_HEAD const byte[:] = "<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8' standalone='yes'?>\n";
let X_TYPES const byte[:] = "<Types xmlns='http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/package/2006/content-types'><Default Extension='rels' ContentType='application/vnd.openxmlformats-package.relationships+xml'/><Default Extension='xml' ContentType='application/xml'/><Default Extension='jpeg' ContentType='image/jpeg'/><Default Extension='png' ContentType='image/png'/><Override PartName='/word/document.xml' ContentType='application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document.main+xml'/><Override PartName='/word/styles.xml' ContentType='application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.styles+xml'/><Override PartName='/word/footer1.xml' ContentType='application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.footer+xml'/><Override PartName='/docProps/core.xml' ContentType='application/vnd.openxmlformats-package.core-properties+xml'/></Types>";
let X_RELS const byte[:] = "<Relationships xmlns='http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/package/2006/relationships'><Relationship Id='rId1' Type='http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/officeDocument' Target='word/document.xml'/><Relationship Id='rId2' Type='http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/package/2006/relationships/metadata/core-properties' Target='docProps/core.xml'/></Relationships>";
let X_CORE const byte[:] = "<cp:coreProperties xmlns:cp='http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/package/2006/metadata/core-properties' xmlns:dc='http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/' xmlns:dcterms='http://purl.org/dc/terms/' xmlns:xsi='http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance'>";
let X_DOCRELS const byte[:] = "<Relationships xmlns='http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/package/2006/relationships'><Relationship Id='rId1' Type='http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/styles' Target='styles.xml'/><Relationship Id='rId2' Type='http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/footer' Target='footer1.xml'/>";
let X_IMGREL const byte[:] = "http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships/image";
let X_STYLES const byte[:] = "<w:styles xmlns:w='http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/wordprocessingml/2006/main'><w:docDefaults><w:rPrDefault><w:rPr><w:rFonts w:ascii='Arial' w:hAnsi='Arial' w:eastAsia='Arial' w:cs='Arial'/><w:color w:val='1C1F24'/><w:sz w:val='20'/><w:szCs w:val='20'/><w:lang w:val='en-US'/></w:rPr></w:rPrDefault><w:pPrDefault><w:pPr><w:spacing w:after='120' w:line='264' w:lineRule='auto'/></w:pPr></w:pPrDefault></w:docDefaults><w:style w:type='paragraph' w:default='1' w:styleId='Normal'><w:name w:val='Normal'/><w:qFormat/></w:style><w:style w:type='paragraph' w:styleId='Title'><w:name w:val='Title'/><w:basedOn w:val='Normal'/><w:next w:val='Normal'/><w:qFormat/><w:pPr><w:spacing w:after='60'/></w:pPr><w:rPr><w:b/><w:sz w:val='40'/><w:szCs w:val='40'/></w:rPr></w:style><w:style w:type='paragraph' w:styleId='Subtitle'><w:name w:val='Subtitle'/><w:basedOn w:val='Normal'/><w:next w:val='Normal'/><w:qFormat/><w:pPr><w:spacing w:after='200'/></w:pPr><w:rPr><w:color w:val='5B6370'/><w:sz w:val='22'/><w:szCs w:val='22'/></w:rPr></w:style><w:style w:type='paragraph' w:styleId='Heading1'><w:name w:val='heading 1'/><w:basedOn w:val='Normal'/><w:next w:val='Normal'/><w:qFormat/><w:pPr><w:keepNext/><w:pBdr><w:bottom w:val='single' w:sz='6' w:space='2' w:color='C89B4E'/></w:pBdr><w:spacing w:before='360' w:after='120'/><w:outlineLvl w:val='0'/></w:pPr><w:rPr><w:b/><w:sz w:val='26'/><w:szCs w:val='26'/></w:rPr></w:style><w:style w:type='paragraph' w:styleId='Heading2'><w:name w:val='heading 2'/><w:basedOn w:val='Normal'/><w:next w:val='Normal'/><w:qFormat/><w:pPr><w:keepNext/><w:spacing w:before='240' w:after='80'/><w:outlineLvl w:val='1'/></w:pPr><w:rPr><w:b/><w:sz w:val='22'/><w:szCs w:val='22'/></w:rPr></w:style><w:style w:type='paragraph' w:styleId='Caption'><w:name w:val='caption'/><w:basedOn w:val='Normal'/><w:next w:val='Normal'/><w:qFormat/><w:pPr><w:spacing w:before='40' w:after='200'/><w:jc w:val='center'/></w:pPr><w:rPr><w:color w:val='5B6370'/><w:sz w:val='17'/><w:szCs w:val='17'/></w:rPr></w:style><w:style w:type='paragraph' w:styleId='Note'><w:name w:val='Note'/><w:basedOn w:val='Normal'/><w:qFormat/><w:rPr><w:i/><w:color w:val='5B6370'/><w:sz w:val='17'/><w:szCs w:val='17'/></w:rPr></w:style><w:style w:type='paragraph' w:styleId='Footer'><w:name w:val='footer'/><w:basedOn w:val='Normal'/><w:pPr><w:spacing w:after='0'/></w:pPr><w:rPr><w:color w:val='5B6370'/><w:sz w:val='16'/><w:szCs w:val='16'/></w:rPr></w:style><w:style w:type='table' w:default='1' w:styleId='TableNormal'><w:name w:val='Normal Table'/><w:tblPr><w:tblInd w:w='0' w:type='dxa'/><w:tblCellMar><w:top w:w='0' w:type='dxa'/><w:left w:w='108' w:type='dxa'/><w:bottom w:w='0' w:type='dxa'/><w:right w:w='108' w:type='dxa'/></w:tblCellMar></w:tblPr></w:style></w:styles>";
let X_DOC const byte[:] = "<w:document xmlns:w='http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/wordprocessingml/2006/main' xmlns:r='http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships' xmlns:wp='http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/wordprocessingDrawing' xmlns:a='http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/main' xmlns:pic='http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/picture'><w:body>";
let X_FTR const byte[:] = "<w:ftr xmlns:w='http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/wordprocessingml/2006/main' xmlns:r='http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships'>";
let X_TBL const byte[:] = "<w:tblPr><w:tblW w:w='5000' w:type='pct'/><w:tblBorders><w:bottom w:val='single' w:sz='4' w:space='0' w:color='D5D9DF'/><w:insideH w:val='single' w:sz='4' w:space='0' w:color='D5D9DF'/></w:tblBorders><w:tblLayout w:type='fixed'/><w:tblCellMar><w:top w:w='60' w:type='dxa'/><w:left w:w='80' w:type='dxa'/><w:bottom w:w='60' w:type='dxa'/><w:right w:w='80' w:type='dxa'/></w:tblCellMar><w:tblLook w:val='04A0'/></w:tblPr>";
let X_TBLPLAIN const byte[:] = "<w:tblPr><w:tblW w:w='5000' w:type='pct'/><w:tblLayout w:type='fixed'/><w:tblLook w:val='0000'/></w:tblPr>";
let H_CSS const byte[:] = "body{margin:0;background:#fff;color:#1c1f24;font:15px/1.5 system-ui,-apple-system,'Segoe UI',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif}main{max-width:8.5in;margin:0 auto;padding:28px 20px 48px}h1{font-size:26px;line-height:1.2;margin:0 0 4px}.sub{color:#5b6370;margin:0 0 12px}.rule{height:3px;background:#c89b4e;border-radius:2px;margin:14px 0 18px}h2{font-size:19px;margin:28px 0 10px;padding-bottom:4px;border-bottom:1px solid #c89b4e}h3{font-size:16px;margin:18px 0 6px}p{margin:0 0 10px}.note{color:#5b6370;font-size:13px;font-style:italic}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;margin:6px 0 16px;font-size:13.5px}th,td{text-align:left;padding:5px 8px;border-bottom:1px solid #dfe2e7;vertical-align:top}thead th{background:#eef0f3;border-bottom:1px solid #8a919c}tbody tr:nth-child(even){background:#f7f8fa}td.n,th.n{text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}table.kv td:first-child{font-weight:600;width:38%}.cap{font-weight:600;font-size:13.5px;margin:14px 0 2px}.verdict{border-left:4px solid;border-radius:4px;padding:9px 12px;margin:10px 0 14px;font-weight:600}.ok{background:#e5f5ec;border-color:#2e9e5b}.warn{background:#fdf3dc;border-color:#c98a0b}.err{background:#fde7e7;border-color:#d33a3a}.figs{display:grid;gap:12px;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(220px,1fr));margin:10px 0 16px}figure{margin:0}figure img,figure svg{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;border:1px solid #d5d9df;border-radius:4px;background:#fff}figure.scale svg{border:0;max-width:360px}figcaption{color:#5b6370;font-size:13px;text-align:center;margin-top:4px}nav.toc{background:#f7f8fa;border-radius:6px;padding:10px 16px;margin:0 0 18px;font-size:14px}nav.toc ol{margin:4px 0 0;padding-left:22px}nav.toc a{color:#1c1f24}footer{margin-top:32px;padding-top:8px;border-top:1px solid #dfe2e7;color:#5b6370;font-size:12.5px;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap}@media print{main{max-width:none;padding:0}h2,h3,.cap{break-after:avoid}figure,tr,.verdict{break-inside:avoid}nav.toc{display:none}}";
```

Word paragraphs go to the scratch `PART`. `w_run(s bytes, bold bool, italic bool, size int)` writes s as runs: each line-feed-separated piece is `<w:r>`, then when bold, italic or size is set `<w:rPr>` with `<w:b/>`, `<w:i/>` and `<w:sz w:val='N'/>` (N is size, a half-point count, written only when above 0) and `</w:rPr>`, then `<w:t xml:space='preserve'>`, the piece with `xesc`, `</w:t></w:r>`; between pieces `<w:r><w:br/></w:r>`. `w_par(s bytes, style bytes, bold bool, center bool, size int)` writes `<w:p>`, `<w:pPr>` with `<w:pStyle w:val='STYLE'/>` when style is not empty and `<w:jc w:val='center'/>` when center, `</w:pPr>`, the runs, `</w:p>`.

`w_cell(text bytes, tw int, fill int, bold bool, right bool)` writes `<w:tc><w:tcPr><w:tcW w:w='TW' w:type='dxa'/>`, then `<w:shd w:val='clear' w:color='auto' w:fill='RRGGBB'/>` when fill is not -1 (`hex6`), `</w:tcPr><w:p><w:pPr><w:spacing w:after='0'/>`, `<w:jc w:val='right'/>` when right, `</w:pPr>`, the runs at size 18 (bold as given) and `</w:p></w:tc>`. `w_table(cols int, rows int, kv bool, cap bytes)`: when cap is not empty add 1 to NTAB and write a paragraph "Table ", NTAB, ". " and cap, bold, size 18, with `<w:keepNext/>` in its properties. Compute `col_widths(cols, rows, kv, CW)` and the twips widths (each TW times 20 rounded). Write `<w:tbl>`, `X_TBL`, `<w:tblGrid>` with `<w:gridCol w:w='TW'/>` per column, `</w:tblGrid>`; when cols has elements and kv is false a header row `<w:tr><w:trPr><w:tblHeader/></w:trPr>` with bold cells on 0xEEF0F3 (right-aligned for numeric columns) and `</w:tr>`; then each body row `<w:tr>` with cells filled 0xF7F8FA on odd rows (else -1), bold for the first column of a key-value table, right-aligned for numeric columns, `</w:tr>`; then `</w:tbl>` and an empty paragraph `<w:p><w:pPr><w:spacing w:after='60'/></w:pPr></w:p>`.

`w_img(k int, cx int, cy int)` writes an inline picture of image k in IM, with cx and cy its size in EMU (points times 12700, rounded) and `WID` a module counter that gives every picture a new id starting at 1: `<w:r><w:drawing><wp:inline distT='0' distB='0' distL='0' distR='0'><wp:extent cx='CX' cy='CY'/><wp:docPr id='ID' name='Picture ID'/><a:graphic><a:graphicData uri='http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/picture'><pic:pic><pic:nvPicPr><pic:cNvPr id='ID' name='image'/><pic:cNvPicPr/></pic:nvPicPr><pic:blipFill><a:blip r:embed='rIdR'/><a:stretch><a:fillRect/></a:stretch></pic:blipFill><pic:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x='0' y='0'/><a:ext cx='CX' cy='CY'/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst='rect'><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom></pic:spPr></pic:pic></a:graphicData></a:graphic></wp:inline></w:drawing></w:r>` with R equal to k plus 10.

`w_fig(b int)` uses the sizes and figure numbers `pdf_fig` would use. One image: a centered paragraph holding `w_img` (its paragraph properties also hold `<w:keepNext/>` when it has a caption, so the caption stays with it), then (when it has a caption) a paragraph in style "Caption" with "Figure n. cap". Several images: `<w:tbl>`, `X_TBLPLAIN`, a grid of n equal columns (cell width in twips), one `<w:tr>` whose cells (`<w:tc><w:tcPr><w:tcW w:w='TW' w:type='dxa'/></w:tcPr>`) each hold a centered paragraph with the picture and, when captioned, a "Caption" paragraph, `</w:tc>`; then `</w:tr></w:tbl>`. `w_pic(b int, wpt real, hpt real, cap bytes)` is for a chart or scale: when the block has a "png" reference, a centered paragraph with its picture at wpt by hpt points; else a "Note" paragraph "(picture not available)"; then the caption paragraph when cap is not empty. A chart is CW by 220 points (its caption numbered like `pdf_chart`), a scale is the smaller of CW and 360 by 42 points with no caption. For a scale the picture paragraph's properties also hold `<w:keepNext/>`, so the scale stays on the page with the figure after it.

`w_verdict(b int)` writes `<w:p><w:pPr><w:pBdr><w:left w:val='single' w:sz='24' w:space='8' w:color='BAR'/></w:pBdr><w:shd w:val='clear' w:color='auto' w:fill='FILL'/><w:spacing w:before='120' w:after='120'/><w:ind w:left='160' w:right='160'/></w:pPr>`, the text as bold runs and `</w:p>`, with the colors of `pdf_verdict`.

`write_docx()` writes the Word document into OUT as a zip. First the document body into PART (emptied): `X_HEAD`, `X_DOC`, the title paragraph (style "Title"), the subtitle (style "Subtitle", when not empty), the meta rows with `w_table(-1, meta, true, "")` when there are any, then the blocks in order: "h" a "Heading1" paragraph with NSEC (after adding 1), ". " and the text; "h2" a "Heading2" paragraph; "p" a plain paragraph; "note" a "Note" paragraph; "verdict" `w_verdict`; "kv" `w_table(-1, rows, true, "")`; "table" `w_table` with "cols", "rows", false and "cap"; "fig" `w_fig`; "chart" and "scale" `w_pic`; "br" `<w:p><w:r><w:br w:type='page'/></w:r></w:p>`. Then the section properties: `<w:sectPr><w:footerReference w:type='default' r:id='rId2'/><w:pgSz w:w='PGW' w:h='PGH'/><w:pgMar w:top='1080' w:right='1080' w:bottom='1080' w:left='1080' w:header='540' w:footer='540' w:gutter='0'/></w:sectPr></w:body></w:document>` with PGW and PGH 12240 and 15840 for Letter or 11906 and 16838 for A4. Add it as "word/document.xml". Then, each built in PART and added: "[Content_Types].xml" (`X_HEAD` and `X_TYPES`), "_rels/.rels" (`X_HEAD`, `X_RELS`), "docProps/core.xml" (`X_HEAD`, `X_CORE`, `<dc:title>` escaped title `</dc:title><dc:creator>Amni-CAD</dc:creator>`, and when the stamp has 14 characters `<dcterms:created xsi:type='dcterms:W3CDTF'>YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ssZ</dcterms:created>`, then `</cp:coreProperties>`), "word/styles.xml" (`X_HEAD`, `X_STYLES`), "word/footer1.xml" (`X_HEAD`, `X_FTR`, `<w:p><w:pPr><w:pStyle w:val='Footer'/><w:tabs><w:tab w:val='right' w:pos='TAB'/></w:tabs></w:pPr>`, the escaped "footer" text as a run, `<w:r><w:tab/></w:r><w:r><w:t xml:space='preserve'>Page </w:t></w:r><w:fldSimple w:instr=' PAGE '><w:r><w:t>1</w:t></w:r></w:fldSimple><w:r><w:t xml:space='preserve'> of </w:t></w:r><w:fldSimple w:instr=' NUMPAGES '><w:r><w:t>1</w:t></w:r></w:fldSimple></w:p></w:ftr>` with TAB the content width in twips: PGW minus 2160) and "word/_rels/document.xml.rels" (`X_HEAD`, `X_DOCRELS`, then per image k `<Relationship Id='rIdR' Type='X_IMGREL' Target='media/imageK.EXT'/>` with R equal to k plus 10 and EXT "png" or "jpeg", then `</Relationships>`). Each image k is added as "word/media/imageK.EXT" straight from its IMG bytes. Finish with `zip_end()`.

`write_html()` writes one self-contained HTML page into OUT. `hesc` is `xesc` with every line feed written as `<br>`. Start: `<!doctype html>` and a line feed, `<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="generator" content="Amni-CAD"><title>`, the escaped title, `</title><style>`, `H_CSS`, `</style></head><body><main><h1>`, title, `</h1>`, then `<p class="sub">` subtitle `</p>` when there is one, the meta rows as `<table class="kv"><tbody>` with `<tr><td>label</td><td>value</td></tr>` rows and `</tbody></table>`, then `<div class="rule"></div>`. When the blocks hold two or more "h" headings, a contents list: `<nav class="toc"><b>Contents</b><ol>` with `<li><a href="#sN">text</a></li>` per heading (N counting from 1) and `</ol></nav>`. Then each block: "h" `<h2 id="sN">N. text</h2>`; "h2" `<h3>text</h3>`; "p" `<p>text</p>`; "note" `<p class="note">text</p>`; "verdict" `<div class="verdict LEVEL">text</div>` (LEVEL is "ok", "warn" or "err"; anything else "warn"); "kv" a `kv` table as above; "table": when captioned `<div class="cap">Table n. cap</div>`, then `<table><thead><tr>` with `<th>` cells (class "n" on numeric columns from `col_widths` at CW) `</tr></thead><tbody>` and `<tr>` rows of `<td>` cells (class "n" on numeric columns) and `</tbody></table>`; "fig" `<div class="figs">` with one `<figure><img src="data:image/jpeg;base64,` (or `image/png` for PNG) followed by the image bytes in base64 (`base64::encode`), `" width="W" height="H" alt="` escaped cap `">` and, when captioned, `<figcaption>Figure n. cap</figcaption>`, `</figure>` per image, then `</div>`; "scale" `<figure class="scale">`, `dl_svg` of `scale_dl(b, CW)` (not standalone, width `DW` and height 42), `</figure>`; "chart" `<div class="figs"><figure>`, `dl_svg` of `chart_dl(b, CW, 220)` (not standalone), a figcaption when captioned, `</figure></div>`; "br" `<div style="break-after:page"></div>`. Figure and table numbers count like the PDF. End with `<footer><span>`, the escaped "footer", `</span><span>Made with Amni-CAD</span></footer></main></body></html>` and a line feed.

CSV. `csv_field(out (a byte list it appends to), s bytes)` writes s as one field: when s holds a comma, a double quote, a carriage return or a line feed, or begins or ends with a space, it is wrapped in double quotes with every double quote doubled; otherwise as is. Fields are separated by commas and lines end with a carriage return and a line feed. Both CSV formats start with the UTF-8 byte order mark (239, 187, 191) so spreadsheets read the units correctly. `num7(out (a byte list it appends to), v real)` writes a number with 7 significant digits: NaN or infinite gives nothing; 0 gives "0"; otherwise with e the power of ten of the leading digit (found by comparing the magnitude with powers of ten, not with a logarithm) it uses std `format_flt` with 6 minus e decimals (clamped from 0 to 15), then drops trailing zeros after the point and a trailing point, and turns "-0" into "0".

`write_csv()` (the summary): the title as one line, the subtitle as one line when present, each meta row as two fields, then for each block: "h" an empty line and the numbered heading as one field; "h2" the text as one field; "verdict" the two fields "Result" and the text; "kv" each row as two fields; "table" the caption line "Table n. cap" when captioned, the header fields, then each row's fields; "chart" for each series a caption line "Figure n. cap" (the series name in parentheses after it when named), a header line of "xl" and "yl", then one line per point with `num7` of x and y; other kinds are skipped. `write_data()` writes the "data" object: its "cols" as the header line, then "rows" lines of the column count `num7` values each, value c of row r at `DAT`[r times the column count plus c]; when there is no data or the buffer is too short for the rows, it writes only the header (an empty line without columns).

Exports. `main()` does nothing. `export fn rp_make(fmt i32) i32`: when `prepare()` fails it refreshes the views and returns -1; otherwise it empties OUT and writes format 1 with `write_pdf()`, 2 `write_docx()`, 3 `write_html()`, 4 `write_csv()` and 5 `write_data()` (any other format writes "report: unknown format" and returns -1), refreshes the views and returns OUT.len. `export fn rp_svg(k i32) i32`: after `prepare()`, finds block k; for a "chart" it runs `chart_dl(b, CW, 220)` and writes `dl_svg` standalone at CW by 220; for a "scale" it runs `scale_dl(b, CW)` and writes it standalone at `DW` by 42; otherwise it returns -1; it refreshes the views and returns OUT.len. `export fn rp_in`, `export fn rp_img` and `export fn rp_dat` are as described at the top.
